Tutorial 2.3 | Equilibrium

- Let's do a little simulation.
  
  - We have a room full of buyers and sellers.
  - Each has their own supply curve and demand curve
  - Each buyer and seller interacts with others in the room
  - Each person talks with one other person. When they interact, they decide what price and how much to buy/sell.
  - If they can agree, they make a contingent deal.
  - Next round, they move on and talk to another person.
  - If they can form a better deal with this new person, they cancel their last contingent deal, and form a new contingent deal.
- We’ve done something like this in classes in past semesters
- This type of outcome is what you might see at farmers markets. You just won’t see the process of arriving, since that has already happened.
- Equilibrium is the place when no one has an incentive to change their price/contingent deal.
- Talk about how quantity demanded equals quantity supplied, that this is from a single price in the market. Talk about how understanding qs and qd will make understanding government policies easier.
- Talk through how this relates to the supply-demand model.

